    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While a sidecar or background process *could* cause resource contention, Kubernetes containers are isolated per PID namespace, so a external process generally cannot directly kill your specific container unless it competes for high-level system limits like total node memory or CPU quotas. To isolate the issue, start by analyzing any `dmesg` output or node logs containing `kubepods` to rule out node-level pressure or cgroups misconfiguration, then force a restart of your affected pods to catch any transient \"noise\" from background processes.",
